The discussion revolves around estimating the distribution of population total incidence based on individual death rates. James inquired about the distribution of total incidence as a function of population levels, leading to an explanation of different statistical models: Poisson and Bernoulli/binomial processes. The conversation then focused on the death rates, establishing a Beta–Binomial framework due to uncertainty in individual death probabilities. Key points included the characteristics of the Beta–Binomial distribution, its means, and variances. James subsequently asked about modeling this distribution via a generalized linear model (GLM), which opened a discussion about two contexts: using the Beta–Binomial as a likelihood in overdispersed situations or as a prior predictive distribution. Recommendations included specific GLM approaches and software implementations for estimating these models effectively.
